ming  techniques coming from mathematical  optimization  to solve statistics problems that's  something i'm happy to talk to you about  it seems it's seen a great deal of  use in algorithm designs for  computational statistics over the last  uh five years or so  so i thought i would give you a really  quick example of  a result of mine from now from a couple  of years ago using this sum of squares  method to solve a very basic problem in  computational statistics and even though  this result is now a couple of years old  it's inspired a lot of work of  the things that i've worked on actually  some things that others have done  using the techniques to design  algorithms for  less simple situations so  um  this is a result  for a very simple problem i want to  estimate the mean of a high dimensional  random vector so i'm from independent  samples so there's some probability  distribution d in high dimensions like  little d dimensions i get some n  independent samples from it i want to  estimate its mean and all i'm going to  assume about this distribution is that  it has bounded covariance so it might  not even have a third moment the third  moment might be infinite  um  and uh  i would like to estimate  the mean by some estimator mu hat and i  would like to get small confidence  intervals for for the mean so i'd like  to i'd like mu hat to be close to mu uh  with good probability with probability  like 1 minus delta and the reason for  this funny expression this is the  distance that you should be is that this  is the distance that you would be to the  mean if you made a stronger assumption  the vector is gaussian and you use the  empirical mean which would be the  optimal estimator so these are the best  confidence intervals that you can hope  for and the question is can you do this  while making much fewer assumptions on  the underlying random variable if you  just allow just assume that it is  bounded covariance and you know the  empirical mean won't do it  right 